o billion dollars other airlines are  also pinching pounds Southwest is  rolling out slimline seats in its new  737 max fleet they're lighter and take  up less space the low fare carrier saved  148 thousand gallons of fuel by changing  how its  the galleys and dropping glass bottles  for cans giving pilots tablets instead  of paper manuals saves 80 pounds of  flight that doesn't sound like much but  it translates to nearly five hundred and  seventy six thousand gallons of fuel a  year that's more than a million dollars  in savings annually  JetBlue saving weight with its new  in-flight entertainment system it'll  feature lighter components that take up  less storage space spirit went with  lighter seats and this smaller tray  table that also allows more rows of  seats to fit on boards it all gets down  to weight CBS News travel editor Peter  Greenberg doing all this doesn't make  the planes any faster and it certainly  doesn't make them any more comfortable  in fact it's just about fuel efficiency  and fuel savings and that just gets back  to the airlines there's no real  passenger benefit but United says it's  more than just dollars United alone uses  almost four billion gallons so anytime  we can reduce our fuel use that goes to  write to our bottom line from an  environmental perspective which is  reducing our emissions it's reducing our  carbon footprint fuel is basically 99%  of an airline's carbon footprint the 737  max is 14% more fuel-efficient than its  predecessors in part because of this  thing on the end of the wing it's called  a winglet it reduces drag for the plane  burns less fuel and later this summer  the airline plans to completely strip  down one of its planes looking for  anywhere else it can save weight and  thus fuel for CBS this morning Kris Van  Cleave Houston wow they're gonna say  gotta leave your iPad behind that extra  weight
 

